  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 1.1. Increased institutional capacity of employer and business membership organizations (EBMOs)</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Business Unity South Africa (BUSA) in partnership with its sector association, the Tourism Business Council of South Africa (TBCSA), undertook in July 2022 a Market Systems Analysis (MSA) measuring the adverse impacts of the COVID-19 Pandemic on the tourism sector. and recommending key policy reforms required to make South Africa a preferred destination of choice for tourism. The analysis used qualitative data collected through informant interviews and focused group discussions from over 800 tourism enterprises, mostly SMEs in 6 major South African tourism destinations. 
Based on the findings of the MSA, BUSA and TBCSA developed a position paper detailing the opportunities  and challenges for the growth and resilience of small tourism businesses in South Africa. In November 2022, TBCSA in partnership with South Africa&#x2019;s leading media, Business Day, launched an advocacy campaign during the high-level public-private sector dialogue on the tourism sector. Through bilateral engagements with the government, particularly the Ministry of Tourism and the Ministry of Home Affairs, they advocated for major policy and regulatory reforms aimed at easing visa restrictions for tourists and improving the business environment for small tourism businesses, particularly enhancing access to credit. In response, the government expanded the e-visa system from 14 countries to 34 countries.  Encouraged, BUSA and TBCSA presented their position paper in the 2023 Tourism Indaba and at the Tourism Leadership Conference of September 2023 making strong cases for reforming the business environment for small tourism enterprises particularly highlighting the importance of building advanced infrastructure for e-visa, removing barriers to access credit, easing the issuance of tourism vehicle operating licenses and ensuring safety and security of tourists. These issues were also at the core of the discussion in the TBCSA&#x2019;s Annual Tourism Leadership Conference, of 6-8 September 2023. The ILO supported the TBCSA in developing the scope of work for the COVID-19 impact assessment on the tourism sector and provided technical guidance on the assessment&#x2019;s methodology. The ILO further assisted the TBCSA validate the assessment by organizing a workshop in July 2022 which involved 50 tourism enterprises.  
To support this assessment, the ILO through its project on Resilient and Sustainable Enterprises in Tourism (RESET) provided extensive support to BUSA and TBCSA to develop a business resilience policy tool kit. Importantly, the ILO built the capacity of TBCSA by working closely with the CEO of    TBCSA and Social Policy Director of BUSA to analyse the results of the Market Systems Analysis  and use the results to develop evidence-based policy positions for advocacy. Through this process, the ILO guided BUSA and TBCSA on how to communicate the results of the study to policy makers and their wider membership via the widely published Business Day Dialogue which attracted 481 YouTube and 967 Facebook Viewership.
Further the ILO developed guidelines   for public-private dialogues that can be used by the sector in future.</narrative>
   </description>
